  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why are planes pressurised?",
      "a": "At about 11 kilometres the air is so thin that people would pass out within a minute or so. Pumping the cabin up to the pressure found about 2,000 metres up lets everyone breathe normally.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do planes need cabin pressure?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does cabin air come from?",
      "a": "On most airliners it is bled from the engines' compressors, then cooled and cleaned. The Boeing 787 instead uses electric compressors that draw in outside air.",
      "asks": [
        "What is bleed air?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the outflow valve?",
      "a": "It is a door-like valve in the fuselage that lets air leave the cabin. By opening and closing it, a controller sets how much air stays inside, and so the cabin pressure.",
      "asks": [
        "How is cabin pressure controlled?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do air conditioning packs do?",
      "a": "Air from the compressors can be over 200 degrees Celsius. The packs cool it, by passing it through heat exchangers and expanding it through a small turbine, before it enters the cabin.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is plane air so dry?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do my ears pop on a plane?",
      "a": "As the plane climbs and descends, cabin pressure changes slowly. Air behind your eardrum evens out through a small tube to your throat, and the pop is that tube opening.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do ears hurt when landing?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Rules generally require cabin pressure equal to an altitude of no more than 8,000 feet, about 2,400 metres.",
    "On many airliners roughly half the cabin air is recirculated through HEPA filters and the rest is fresh outside air."
  ],
